        "kind": "definition",
        "title": "Misfit evidence and the potential posterior law",
        "statement": "Let $X$ be a separable real Banach space with its norm topology, $D\\subseteq X$ a Borel forward-map domain, and $\\mu_0$ a Borel probability measure on $X$ with $\\mu_0(D)=1$. Write $\\mu_{0,D}$ for its restriction to $D$, so $\\mu_{0,D}(A)=\\mu_0(A)$ for $A\\in\\mathcal B(D):=\\{D\\cap B:B\\in\\mathcal B(X)\\}$. Let $(\\mathsf Y,\\mathcal Y)$ be a measurable data space. A likelihood potential is a function $\\Phi:D\\times\\mathsf Y\\to(-\\infty,+\\infty]$ that is measurable from $\\bigl(D\\times\\mathsf Y,\\mathcal B(D)\\otimes\\mathcal Y\\bigr)$ to $\\bigl((-\\infty,+\\infty],\\mathcal B((-\\infty,+\\infty])\\bigr)$, where $\\mathcal B((-\\infty,+\\infty])$ is the trace of $\\mathcal B(\\overline{\\mathbb R})$ on $(-\\infty,+\\infty]$. Viewing $\\Phi$ via the inclusion $(-\\infty,+\\infty]\\hookrightarrow\\overline{\\mathbb R}$, it is also product-measurable as an extended-real-valued map because for every Borel set $B\\subseteq\\overline{\\mathbb R}$, $\\Phi^{-1}(B)=\\Phi^{-1}(B\\cap(-\\infty,+\\infty])$ is measurable by the definition of the trace sigma-algebra. Here $+\\infty$ is allowed and $-\\infty$ is excluded; set $e^{-\\infty}=0$. The scalar map $t\\mapsto e^{-t}$ on this range is continuous at finite $t$, and is continuous at $+\\infty$: given $\\varepsilon>0$, the limit supplies $a\\in\\mathbb R$ such that $t>a$ implies $e^{-t}<\\varepsilon$, and the order-topology neighborhood $(a,+\\infty]$ maps into $[0,\\varepsilon)$. Hence it is Borel, so $L_\\Phi:D\\times\\mathsf Y\\to[0,+\\infty)$, $L_\\Phi(u;y):=e^{-\\Phi(u;y)}$, is product-measurable by composition, and each section $u\\mapsto L_\\Phi(u;y)$ is nonnegative and measurable. Define its evidence by",
